  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An isolated shower or storm can't be ruled out in \nthe south this afternoon, but much of the area should stay dry.\n\nWeak high pressure is currently building into the area from the NE, \nas a backdoor cold front sinks into central SC. This front is \nfarther south than anticipated, which is keeping any deep convection \nwell to our south as well. Even the sea breeze convection is staying \npinned to far SE NC. CAMS have trended drier for today as well. So \nwhile a stray shower or storm can't be ruled out this afternoon over \nour southern zones where 1000-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE is currently in \nplace according to SPC mesoanalysis, no severe weather is \nanticipated, and much of the area will stay dry. Thus lowered POPs \nto mainly slight chance, ending by 00z. Otherwise, enough low-level \nmoisture should still be in place for some patchy fog and low \nstratus early Sunday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Scattered to numerous showers are expected Monday \nand Tuesday. Strong to severe thunderstorms are possible Monday, \nalong with locally excessive rainfall both days.\n\nA cold front will parallel the Virginia/North Carolina border Monday \nmorning and will slowly sink south into North Carolina on Monday. \nDepending on how long it takes for the front to sink south, the \nwestern half of North Carolina could remain on the warm side of the \nfront long enough to realize some instability and have some strong \nto severe thunderstorms develop. However, the greater threat is \nlikely to come from strong rainfall considering storm motion is \nexpected to be slow along the front. The Weather Prediction Center \ncontinues to have all of central NC in a Marginal Risk (level 1 of \n4) for excessive rainfall both days. Monday appears to be the last \nhot day for some time across the area - while highs will range from \nthe mid 80s to the low 90s on Monday, highs will range from the mid \n70s to the mid 80s on Tuesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Cold-air damming is likely to result in well below \nnormal temperatures through the second half of the week, bringing \nmuch more fall-like weather.\n\nHigh pressure over Canada will drift east, currently in Manitoba, \nbut eventually sliding to Quebec by Wednesday and then remaining \nstationary through the rest of the week. The models still have not \ncome into much agreement as to whether a surface low will develop \nalong the Atlantic coastline and move north towards New England, but \neither way, a ridge of high pressure should extend south from Canada \nas far south as Georgia at least on Wednesday and Thursday, if not \nlonger. This should keep the weather cool. The current forecast high \nfor Greensboro on Wednesday is 69 degrees, and the last time \nGreensboro had a high temperature below 70 degrees was May 23. A few \nlocations could even drop into the upper 40s by Saturday morning. \nAll locations will have a chance for scattered showers or drizzle on \nWednesday and Thursday, with Friday and Saturday looking drier.",
